{"subjectId":"javascript","id":"data-types","title":"Data Types & typeof","group":"Getting Started","order":4,"html":"<p class=\"lead\">Seven primitive types plus objects. Values have types; variables do not.</p>\n<section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-category\"></i>The eight types</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Type</th><th>Examples</th><th><code>typeof</code></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Number</strong></td><td><code>42</code>, <code>3.14</code>, <code>-0</code>, <code>NaN</code>, <code>Infinity</code></td><td><code>\"number\"</code></td></tr><tr><td><strong>BigInt</strong></td><td><code>9007199254740993n</code></td><td><code>\"bigint\"</code></td></tr><tr><td><strong>String</strong></td><td><code>'hi'</code>, <code>\"hi\"</code>, template literals with backticks</td><td><code>\"string\"</code></td></tr><tr><td><strong>Boolean</strong></td><td><code>true</code>, <code>false</code></td><td><code>\"boolean\"</code></td></tr><tr><td><strong>Undefined</strong></td><td><code>undefined</code> — declared but no value</td><td><code>\"undefined\"</code></td></tr><tr><td><strong>Null</strong></td><td><code>null</code> — intentional “no value”</td><td><code>\"object\"</code> (historic bug!)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Symbol</strong></td><td><code>Symbol('id')</code> — unique key</td><td><code>\"symbol\"</code></td></tr><tr><td><strong>Object</strong></td><td><code>{}</code>, <code>[]</code>, functions, dates, maps…</td><td><code>\"object\"</code> / <code>\"function\"</code></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-search\"></i><code>typeof</code> in practice</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"js\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>JavaScript</figcaption><pre><code>console.log(typeof 42, typeof 10n, typeof \"s\", typeof true);\nconsole.log(typeof undefined, typeof Symbol(), typeof {}, typeof []);\nconsole.log(typeof null);                 // the famous bug\nconsole.log(typeof function () {}, typeof class {});\nconsole.log(typeof notDeclaredAnywhere);  // no ReferenceError</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>number bigint string boolean\nundefined symbol object object\nobject\nfunction function\nundefined</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"callout callout--warn\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Watch out</div><p><code>typeof null === \"object\"</code> is a bug from JavaScript's first implementation, kept for backward compatibility. Test for null with <code>value === null</code>.</p></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-checks\"></i>Checking types reliably</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Question</th><th>Use</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Is it an array?</td><td><code>Array.isArray(x)</code> (not <code>typeof</code>)</td></tr><tr><td>Is it null?</td><td><code>x === null</code></td></tr><tr><td>Null or undefined?</td><td><code>x == null</code> (the one accepted use of <code>==</code>) or <code>x ?? fallback</code></td></tr><tr><td>Instance of a class?</td><td><code>x instanceof Date</code> (walks the prototype chain)</td></tr><tr><td>Exact built-in tag?</td><td><code>Object.prototype.toString.call(x)</code></td></tr><tr><td>Is it NaN?</td><td><code>Number.isNaN(x)</code></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<figure data-lang=\"js\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>JavaScript</figcaption><pre><code>const tag = (x) =&gt; Object.prototype.toString.call(x);\nconsole.log(Array.isArray([]), [] instanceof Array);\nconsole.log(tag(null), tag([]), tag(new Date(0)), tag(/re/));</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>true true\n[object Null] [object Array] [object Date] [object RegExp]</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-circle-off\"></i><code>undefined</code> vs <code>null</code></h3>\n<div class=\"grid\"><div class=\"card\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-question-mark\"></i><code>undefined</code></h3><p>The engine's “nothing here”: an unassigned variable, a missing property, a missing argument, a function with no <code>return</code>.</p></div><div class=\"card\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-circle-off\"></i><code>null</code></h3><p>The programmer's “nothing here”: an intentional empty value you assign yourself.</p></div></div>\n<figure data-lang=\"js\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>JavaScript</figcaption><pre><code>let a;\nconst obj = {};\nfunction f() {}\nconsole.log(a, obj.missing, f());\nconsole.log(null == undefined, null === undefined);\nconsole.log(Number(null), Number(undefined));</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>undefined undefined undefined\ntrue false\n0 NaN</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-arrows-shuffle\"></i>Dynamic typing</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"js\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>JavaScript</figcaption><pre><code>let value = 10;\nconsole.log(typeof value);\nvalue = \"ten\";\nconsole.log(typeof value);\nvalue = [10];\nconsole.log(typeof value, Array.isArray(value));</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>number\nstring\nobject true</code></pre></figure>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>How many data types does JavaScript have?</summary><div><p>Eight: seven primitives (<code>number</code>, <code>bigint</code>, <code>string</code>, <code>boolean</code>, <code>undefined</code>, <code>null</code>, <code>symbol</code>) and <code>object</code>. Arrays, functions, dates and maps are all objects.</p></div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>Why does typeof null return \"object\"?</summary><div><p>In the first implementation values carried a type tag, and the tag for objects was 0; <code>null</code> was the null pointer (all zeros), so it read as an object. Fixing it would break the web, so it stays.</p></div></details></section>"}